\title{bsfa: Bayesian Stochastic Frontier Analysis}
\description{
Bayesian inference for stochastic frontier models by Markov chain Monte Carlo. Gibbs samplers with data augmentation are provided for composed-error frontier models in the tradition of van den Broeck, Koop, Osiewalski and Steel (1994), covering exponential, half-normal and truncated normal inefficiency, production and cost frontiers, and cross-sectional, time-invariant panel and four-component panel specifications. The scale of an inefficiency term may depend on covariates, as in Caudill, Ford and Gropper (1995), and the pre-truncation mean of a truncated normal one may too, as in Battese and Coelli (1995); those coefficients have no conjugate full conditional and are drawn by Metropolis steps within the sweep. A model is built up step by step, by creating a model object and then adding priors, initial values and a seed to it before the posterior is drawn. Posterior distributions of unit-specific efficiency scores are obtained directly from the augmented draws rather than from point predictors.
}
\references{
Aigner, D., Lovell, C. A. K., & Schmidt, P. (1977). Formulation and
